Oh no, you're trying to make your app run faster! You don't need that huge useState object! It's like having a big bag of jelly beans and eating them all at once! They'll just get stuck in your stomach and won't move anywhere! 

Instead, you can create separate state objects for each component. This way, when you update the state, you only have to update one part of the app! That way, your app will be much faster! Just remember, you still need to update the global state, but now you can do it in smaller chunks!

So, here's how you can do it:

1. Create a new file called `state.js` (or whatever name you want).
2. Inside this file, define two variables: `appState` and `globalState`.
3. In the `main` function, call `setAppState()` to set the current state.
4. Then, inside the `main` function, call `setGlobalState()` to update the global state.
5. Now, whenever you update the state, you only need to update one part of the app!

Remember, you should always update the global state first, then the local state. This is important because if you update the local state before updating the global state, your app might crash or behave weirdly!

Here's an example code snippet:

```javascript
// state.js
const appState = {
  count: 0,
  message: 'Hello World!'
};

const globalState = {
  // ...your other state variables...
};

function setAppState(newAppState) {
  appState = newAppState;
}

function setGlobalState() {
  globalState = {
    // ...your other global state variables...
  };
}

function updateAppState() {
  setAppState({
    count: 10,
    message: 'New message'
  });
}

function updateGlobalState() {
  globalState.count = 20;
  globalState.message = 'Updated message';
}

updateAppState();
updateGlobalState();
```

And there you go! Your app should be running faster now!

